fix: keep authority moving forwards, and land the operator where the page works

The third review round found three P0s on the version lifecycle. All three share one root:
several places each decided for themselves what "the current ruleset version" means.

- Freezing succeeded and then redirected into ``ruleset_edit``, which refuses frozen
  versions: the row froze, the success message was replaced by a 403, and the honest reading
  of the screen was "it failed". The operator now lands on the workspace — the page that can
  answer "and now what?" for a frozen version — and a *refused* freeze still lands back on
  the page where the fix is. The existing tests asserted only "302" and never followed the
  redirect, which is exactly how it stayed hidden.

- A successor draft offered "重置第一轮并继续修改" for a first round the running authority
  owns. Reset is not a step towards changing the number there: it destroys the prepared
  first round's scores and seats, and the frozen-round guard still refuses the new number
  afterwards. That state now offers the clone-and-rebind action instead, and never the reset
  button. The clone copies configuration only — scoring mode, judge counts, rubric, roster
  source — onto a fresh DRAFT round and rebinds the successor to it, so "change 10 to 8"
  is genuinely one save again; the running authority keeps the round it promised.

- ``v1 DRAFT`` beside a ``v2 FROZEN`` was still editable. The workspace preferred *any*
  draft over the authority, and the guards asked only for a newer *draft*, so v1 stayed open
  — and freezing it would have demoted v2 and moved the activity's authority backwards to a
  definition it had already left behind. "Which version may be edited" is now one rule
  (`newer_ruleset_version` / `editable_ruleset_version` / `version_is_editable`) used by the
  workspace, ruleset creation, provisioning, the 2026 pages, the binding editor, the generic
  editor, the definition writers, the questionnaire designer and the planning services. The
  freeze service fails closed on it too, so a page that were bypassed could not invert the
  authority: the UI guard is one rendering of the rule, not the rule.

Also: the freeze button is disabled while a blocker stands (the server refused anyway; this
is about not inviting the click), and the Security workflow now pins the fact its Twisted
``--ignore-vuln`` rests on — the lock resolving 26.4.0 — so a dependency bump fails the
build and forces the exception to be revisited instead of leaving it forever.

Tests: Live2026VersionLifecycleTests covers the post-freeze landing (success and refusal),
the disabled button, the protected round offering clone instead of reset, the clone action
end to end (clone → change seats → the successor's definition and round follow → the freeze
gate reports nothing left), and the older draft beside a frozen successor (workspace shows
the authority, both pages refuse to open it for writing, the service refuses the freeze).
Two existing fixtures had to change: one built "v1 DRAFT + v2 FROZEN" to exercise the
frozen-round guard and now freezes v2 and edits v3, and one left a scratch v99 draft in
front of the version it then froze.

Verified: 2199 tests run, 2198 pass (40 skipped, 1 pre-existing local-only failure in
tests/test_static_smoke.py that fails identically on the unmodified tree); ruff, ruff
format, pyright, mypy, makemigrations --check, check, check_docs and check:css clean.
